<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lutheranism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lutheran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lutheranism is the branch of Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> based on the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, who launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1517. Its spread divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> along religious lines and led to wars ended by settlements such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a>. Lutheranism became the state religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> and gained followers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lutheranism.jpg" alt="Lutheranism" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atleett · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konsekration_Alsike_Kyrka.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word Lutheran began as a term of abuse. The theologian Johann Maier von Eck used it against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> at the Leipzig Debate in July 1519, following the Catholic practice of naming a heresy after its leader. The schism between Roman Catholicism and Lutheranism was formalised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-worms">Edict of Worms</a> of 1521 and centred on two points: the source of authority in the church and the doctrine of justification. Lutherans hold that Scripture is the final authority and that people are saved by grace alone, through faith alone, on the basis of Scripture alone. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, as defined at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>, holds that authority comes from both Scripture and tradition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Lutheran churches adhere to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and the ecumenical creeds, and their doctrine is set out in the Book of Concord, published in 1580. They keep many liturgical practices and sacramental teachings of the pre-Reformation Western Church, with particular emphasis on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, and generally recognise three sacraments: baptism, confession and the Eucharist. Good works are held to be the fruit of faith and not a cause of salvation. Music is central to worship, and Luther himself was a great proponent of it; Lutheran hymns are sometimes called chorales, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> composed a huge body of sacred music for the Lutheran church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Lutheranism became the state church of many parts of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1525. It spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> in the sixteenth century as the monarchs of Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> adopted it, and through Baltic-German and Swedish rule into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and Latvia. Almost all of the Lithuanian nobility converted to Lutheranism or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> lost support there at the end of the seventeenth century. After Luther's death in 1546 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> began in 1547, and imperial forces oppressed and exiled many German Lutherans. Religious freedom was secured for them in some areas by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-passau">Peace of Passau</a> in 1552 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> in 1555, under the principle that the ruler's religion decided that of his subjects.</p><p>From about 1580 to 1730 a period of Lutheran Orthodoxy followed, with scholastic theology developed by Johann Gerhard and others to argue with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>. Sizable Lutheran missions arose in the nineteenth century, though schools were already part of the work begun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1706 by Bartholomew Ziegenbalg. Lutherans now number about 90 million and live on every continent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lutheranism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Unlike their Franconian cousins, who adopted <b>Lutheranism</b>, the Swabian counts and their subjects remained Catholic through the Reformation, and the county lay within the diocese of Constance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Church Order of 1525 made <b>Lutheranism</b> the state church, the first Protestant one, and provided for visitations of parishes, first carried out by Bishop George in 1538; officials toured the duchy enforcing Lutheran teaching and penalizing pagans and dissidents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch Reformed Church was the public church, but Catholics, Mennonites, <b>Lutherans</b> and Jews could worship privately, and Sephardic Jews from Portugal settled in Amsterdam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto Henry introduced <b>Lutheranism</b> in 1556, and Frederick III turned to Reformed Protestantism, ordering images removed from churches and imposing strict church discipline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Peace of Augsburg in 1555 allowed territorial rulers to choose between Catholicism and <b>Lutheranism</b> for their domains, and the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 extended recognition to Calvinism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion shaped society deeply: the Hussite revolution transferred much church land to nobles and towns, and for two centuries Utraquists, who gave communion in both kinds, coexisted with Catholics, later joined by the Unity of the Brethren and <b>Lutherans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
